{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"RSO no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"For RSO-covered units, a landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD before serving any no-fault termination notice, file a copy of the notice within 3 days, and pay relocation assistance within 15 days of service.","key_value":"$11,000–$27,400 per unit (7/1/2026–6/30/2027)","coverage_conditions":"Rental units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance; relocation is paid per unit, not per tenant, based on tenancy length and tenant income.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"RSO-covered rental units; all RSO units are Protected Units"},"exemptions":"Reduced Mom-and-Pop relocation (4 or fewer units, owner holds no more than 4 units, not used in prior 3 years) for owner, relative or resident-manager occupancy; no payment for replacing a resident manager or for natural-disaster evictions not caused by landlord negligence; landlord may offset accrued rent, except for governmental vacate orders.","penalty":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the RSO; the tenant may raise the landlord's failure to provide relocation assistance as an affirmative defense in an eviction action.","effective_date":"2026-07-01","citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §151.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"All tenant not- at-fault evictions require payment of relocation assistance and the filing of a Declaration of Intent to Evict form with the Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) before evicting tenants from units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) or the Just Cause Ordinance (JCO).","interaction":"RSO amounts must be enhanced for lower income tenants under the state Housing Crisis Act; the owner must pay the highest of the applicable State and City relocation amounts.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"JCO no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"For JCO-covered units, a landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD before serving a no-fault notice and pay relocation assistance within 15 days; relocation for an owner's single-family dwelling is one month's rent.","key_value":"$11,000–$27,400 per unit (7/1/2026–6/30/2027); one month's rent for owner's single-family dwelling","coverage_conditions":"Rental units covered by the Just Cause Ordinance, including single-family dwellings owned by a natural person holding no more than four units and a single-family home on a separate lot.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"JCO-covered rental units; single-family dwellings owned by natural persons"},"exemptions":"Reduced Mom-and-Pop relocation for owner, relative or resident-manager occupancy in buildings of 4 or fewer units; no payment for replacing a resident manager or for natural-disaster evictions not caused by landlord negligence; landlord may offset accrued rent.","penalty":"Failure to file the Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the JCO; the tenant may raise the failure to provide relocation assistance as an affirmative defense.","effective_date":"2026-07-01","citation":"Los Angeles Just Cause Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §165.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"Notices to terminate tenancy can only be served on the tenant after the landlord files the Declaration with LAHD, and a copy of the notice must be filed with LAHD no later than 3 days after it is served.","interaction":"JCO amounts must be enhanced for lower income tenants under the state Housing Crisis Act; the owner must pay the highest of the applicable State and City relocation amounts.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Resident Protections Ordinance relocation for demolition of Protected Units","requirement":"An owner demolishing a Protected Unit for new construction within 5 years of filing the notice must provide relocation, pay the highest of State and City amounts, and give lower income tenants enhanced relocation.","key_value":"$250,000 per displaced unit (minimum penalty); Chart B relocation for lower income","coverage_conditions":"Protected Units demolished for new construction: units under rent or price control, affordable covenants, or lower-income occupancy in the past 5 years, or Ellis Act withdrawals in the past 10 years.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Protected Units demolished for new construction; all RSO units are Protected Units"},"exemptions":"The RPO applies only to Protected Units demolished for new construction; regular RSO or JCO relocation rules apply if the units are not Protected Units or are not occupied by a lower income tenant.","penalty":"Failure to comply may result in financial penalties starting at $250,000 per displaced unit and withholding of LAHD permit clearances.","effective_date":"2025-02-11","citation":"Los Angeles Resident Protections Ordinance (Ordinances #188481 and #188482)","quoted_span":"Owners who demolish a Protected Unit for the purpose of new construction within 5 years after the filing of the Notice of Intent to Withdraw or Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD are required to comply with relocation requirements under the RPO. Failure to comply with the RPO may result in financial penalties starting at $250,000 per displaced unit and withholding of LAHD permit clearances.","interaction":"Owner must pay the highest of State and City relocation amounts; RSO/JCO amounts must be enhanced for lower income tenants under the HCA regardless of RPO applicability.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Housing Crisis Act relocation for lower income demolition displacees","requirement":"Under the Housing Crisis Act, only lower income households (80% AMI or lower) displaced by demolition where the owner replaces the units with new construction must receive relocation payments matching the benefits public entities pay under Government Code Chapter 16.","key_value":"Relocation benefits under Gov. Code ch. 16 for households at or below 80% AMI","coverage_conditions":"Lower income households (80% AMI or lower) displaced by demolition of their units where the owner will replace them with new construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Demolition for new construction displacing lower income households"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 66300.6","quoted_span":"ONLY lower income households (80% AMI or lower) being displaced for demolition of their units where the owner will replace the units with new construction receive relocation payment consistent to the relocation benefits required to be paid by public entities under Chapter 16 (commencing with Section 7260) of Division 7 of Title 1 (Government Code 66300.6(b)(4)(A)).","interaction":"HCA amounts apply alongside City RSO/JCO amounts; the owner must pay the highest of the applicable State and City relocation amounts.","confidence":0.65,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
